1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:08,720
Welcome to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,280
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:32,720
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers and grow revenue across iOS, Android and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:43,360
Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard.

7
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:52,000
My guest today is Anata Lakra, co-founder and CEO of Bold Voice, the AI speech coaching app for non-native English speakers.

8
00:00:52,640 --> 00:01:06,560
On the podcast, I talk with Anata about scaling to 10 million in ARR with a team of 10, having every employee do two user interviews a week, and why their hard paywall win backfired.

9
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:09,520
Hey, Anata, thanks so much for joining me on the podcast today.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:10,880
Thank you for having me.

11
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:11,680
I'm pumped.

12
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,080
Yeah, I'm really looking forward to this conversation.

13
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:19,600
So you like so many builders, built something for yourself.

14
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:26,480
You came to the US and you know what, let me I'm about to tell your story.

15
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:39,600
Let me just have you tell the story because I think it's a fantastic story and a fantastic example of how when you face real world problems that you need solutions to, sometimes software is a great solution and that often is the best way to build something.

16
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:41,840
Yeah, so let me go all the way back.

17
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:44,000
I was born and raised in Albania.

18
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:51,600
I lived there for the first eighteen years of my life and I moved to the US for college when I moved here., I had never actually been to the US before.

19
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:52,880
It was my first time visiting.

20
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:57,200
I didn't have any family or friends here, so it was a pretty big move.

21
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,640
And I felt pretty prepared for it at the time because, you know, I applied to colleges, figured out the whole process on my own.

22
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:10,880
I had gone into Yale, which was, you know, especially at the time, a huge accomplishment.

23
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:13,920
And felt, you know, very confident when I first moved here.

24
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:22,000
So imagine my surprise when I started to speak and people were asking me to repeat myself and were clearly not understanding me.

25
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:28,560
And it really, again, surprised me because by that point, I had learned English for ten years and since I was basically ape.

26
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:30,800
So passed all the tests, right?

27
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:31,840
Got A's and everything.

28
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:33,760
So why were people not understanding me?

29
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:42,240
And from that experience, I realized that you can learn a language on paper, but it's actually very different to communicate it confidently in real life.

30
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:50,160
Your pronunciation, your accent, your fluency, your conversational skills, all of those things can get in the way of being clearly understood.

31
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:58,960
And again, I realized there was this big gap and all that learning had not prepared me for that moment that I would actually communicate in a native English speaking environment.

32
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:00,480
And definitely.

33
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:18,640
played a big role at the time, both in classroom settings where I'd raise my hand and say something and people would ask me to repeat myself or later when it came to job interviews where again I would have to think about I'm not actually sure how to say this word, let me think of a synonym on the spot because I don't want to butcher this.

34
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:27,920
So I realized it's a pretty big problem and many, many years later decided to start Bold Voice to help people again at this last mile of fluency.

35
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:39,040
They've done all this learning on paper, but they still maybe struggle to communicate as clearly and be as confident in English as they are in their native language and the job of Bold Voices to fill that gap.

36
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:40,160
Awesome.

37
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:43,840
And then your co-founder kind of has similar story.

38
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:46,480
How did you meet him and what's his, what's his story?

39
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:51,600
His story is that he actually moved to the US when his parent immigrated when he was four.

40
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:57,520
So he's essentially a native speaker, but his parents when they moved were in their thirties.

41
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:10,160
And even though, you know, well educated software engineers, they still face the ceiling of having this accent and feeling like, well, I can't speak as well and as beautifully as my American colleagues.

42
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:20,720
And therefore, I don't feel comfortable managing people and they were never like being offered those managerial opportunities and those promotional opportunities to a great extent due to their communication confidence.

43
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:27,040
So he's seen this from the perspective of his parents, which was clearly, again, very aligned with our mission.

44
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:32,320
And we actually met in both of our first jobs out of college in consulting.

45
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:41,680
And I think we very clearly and quickly both realized that financial services consulting was not what we wanted to build our careers around.

46
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:45,280
And we both wanted to actually build something, do something more entrepreneurial.

47
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:52,480
And so we actually both together left that consulting company to build a startup in a very different space.

48
00:04:53,040 --> 00:05:02,880
But we're both now with Boldfoy's second time found co-founders together, which says a lot about the co-founding relationship and that trust that you need to have with your co-founder.

49
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:03,680
That's awesome.

50
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:06,800
I didn't look deep enough in your LinkedIn, was it McKenzie?

51
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:12,720
And then you just not want to say it or I can't share, can't confirm or deny.

52
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:18,000
You know, it's funny because I feel like there are so many stories though.

53
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:27,920
Some of those consulting companies, you know, they they just have a really high bar to hire and like you can make fun of them and maybe they overcharge for some of their services and maybe they give obvious answers to some folks.

54
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:37,680
But boy, I've I've known a lot of really sharp people who go on to be very successful founders who got their careers started at McKinsey or Accenture or some of these consulting companies.

55
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:42,800
So like, I know there's a little bit of stigma around it, but like, I honestly, like, I just know so many great people.

56
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:44,080
No, but very true.

57
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:46,400
But actually, let me tell you this one funny story.

58
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:53,200
I applied to McKinsey in college and I didn't, I mean, I got the interview, but I didn't get an offer from McKinsey.

59
00:05:53,680 --> 00:06:42,000
And that was one of the cases of those interviews where I felt like, how am I going to, especially when a big part of being a consultant right is that clarity and the communication confidence i struggle to actually communicate my ideas that confidently and i actually got an offer from a swiss consulting firm where my interviewers were non-native speakers so we could get way faster into the core of the ideas as opposed to like the accent and the communication and then when i was in business school about you know five six however many years later i got my offer from mckenzie because by that point i'd worked on my communication and on my accent so it's a kind of a really interesting again another validation point of the problem we're going after in a little bit of a funny full circle moment.

60
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:43,120
That is so fun.

61
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:48,880
I'm glad I asked because that is such a fun story and a perfect example of why you built this company.

62
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:50,800
So then take me through.

63
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:54,000
You were co founders with Ilya is his name, right?

64
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:54,400
Yeah.

65
00:06:54,800 --> 00:07:03,920
So you and Ilya founded a separate company, maybe just speedrun, how that went, but then like, you know, the start up Bold Voice, did you raise a bunch of money?

66
00:07:04,240 --> 00:07:05,840
Like, how did you get that first product in the market?

67
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:06,720
What did that look like?

68
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:12,560
That first startup that we did was a recruiting technology company and we were bringing more data.

69
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:23,040
data analytics primarily through third-party references to employers so they could make better hiring decisions from the start instead of suffering the cost of hiring a person who was not the right fit.

70
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:25,520
We were 23, 24 at the time.

71
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:27,360
It was our first startup.

72
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:34,720
We had barely had any experience, but it was an incredible learning experience of how to build a product, how to talk to users.

73
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:39,040
And in hindsight, there are a lot of, again, the typical mistakes the first time founders made.

74
00:07:39,280 --> 00:07:40,080
We made the same things.

75
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:45,360
We started obsessing with the solution before really understanding the problem and talking to users.

76
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,640
So I learned a lot through that experience that you can just build a product in a vacuum and then bring it to customers.

77
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:54,080
You actually have to involve them from the very beginning of that process.

78
00:07:55,040 --> 00:07:56,720
And we had some traction with it.

79
00:07:56,880 --> 00:08:06,800
We had over a dozen companies using it, thousands of candidates going through it, but we just didn't have quite the momentum to raise a big round at the time and be able to continue with it.

80
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:09,600
So eventually we sunset that company.

81
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:24,000
And I through that experience, though, found my passion in product and in building the product experiences, talking to users, understanding and converting that into like beautiful functional UX and, and again, product features that solve problems.

82
00:08:24,240 --> 00:08:28,960
So with that, I went into a different Series B at the time, startup in New York.

83
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:38,800
Then from there, I went to build product at Peloton, which was really fun, and another kind of consumer facing product that at the time was, you know, exploding.

84
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:42,000
And it was a really fun experience to build product there.

85
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:47,200
And then we started Bold Voice when I was in business school.

86
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:53,840
And I was, again, thinking about, you know, having done a startup before, you realize how difficult it is.

87
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:00,400
You realize that it sounds sexy from the outside, but actually the inner workings are just grinding nonstop.

88
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:04,560
stop and the more you accomplish in a way, the more the intensity ramp up.

89
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:15,600
So I was very picky about exploring ideas because I knew that if I was going to do something again, it needed to be something where I could see myself spending the next decade of my life working on that problem.

90
00:09:16,320 --> 00:09:22,800
And the bold voice idea came to me because again, in a way, it had been in my head for a decade since I first moved here.

91
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:26,000
And it was also inspired by what I was starting to read.

92
00:09:26,240 --> 00:09:27,440
Again, this was 2021.

93
00:09:27,680 --> 00:09:36,720
This was before ChatGPT and some of these latest things that we're seeing now with AI, but already why I'm starting to read more about audio AI becoming more powerful.

94
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:44,000
So the idea there is, can we build something with AI that can hear you as well as a coach would?

95
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:49,760
Because when it comes to speech coaching, accent coaching, you can't really just give people thumbs up, thumbs down.

96
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:52,240
You really need super precise feedback for it to work.

97
00:09:52,960 --> 00:10:05,360
So at the time, it seemed like with how audio AI was progressing, we could build those ears on the machine to actually build a product that could function like a speech coach in your pocket without needing that person in the room.

98
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:07,600
And so that was a thesis.

99
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:12,560
And now we're here five years later and we're still building around that same idea.

100
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:13,200
Yeah.

101
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:15,200
So then did you raise money?

102
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:17,920
Was this a side project where you're at a business school?

103
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:19,680
Did Ilya quit his job?

104
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
Like, what was the actual founding story?

105
00:10:22,720 --> 00:10:22,800
Yeah.

106
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:27,680
So I was six months shy of graduating from Harvard Business School.

107
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:32,400
I decided to say no to my McKinsey return offer so I could focus full time on this.

108
00:10:32,960 --> 00:10:34,320
And I gave myself a deadline.

109
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:36,000
I was graduating with six months.

110
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:39,280
I told myself within those six months, I need a term sheet.

111
00:10:39,920 --> 00:10:43,280
And if I don't have a term sheet, then I can start applying to jobs.

112
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:46,320
But if I do, of course, I'll continue this full time.

113
00:10:47,040 --> 00:10:50,080
And in the meantime, I'm doing this 110%.

114
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:52,640
I'm not entertaining any other projects.

115
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:59,760
So that was a great, that deadline was a great forcing mechanism to have that 100% again focus that I think is needed.

116
00:10:59,840 --> 00:11:04,800
Otherwise, a startup won't happen if your attention is split in a lot of different ways.

117
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:07,200
So got to work very quickly.

118
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:11,040
The first thing that I realized is I'm the user, but I'm not the expert here.

119
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:12,640
I'm not the speech coach.

120
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:18,800
So the first thing I did was to reach out to a bunch of different speech coaches who are experts in the field.

121
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:33,440
I had this idea that if we got Hollywood speech coaches to work with us, that would be especially differentiated and very cool because these are people who work with celebrities and actors and they often also have an acting background themselves.

122
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:36,640
So they would be really great people to have involved in this project.

123
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:40,800
So that was my first thing that I did reach out, explored conversations with them.

124
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:50,800
I also once we had our first coach, Ron, who's incredible and still works with us now five years later, put together a pilot with him where he recorded a bunch of videos.

125
00:11:51,280 --> 00:12:05,280
I found a few people, about a dozen people who wanted to, who were willing to do a beta run of this and we were sending them manually these videos and they were giving us their experien speech recordings back in emails and the coach was giving them feedback asynchronously.

126
00:12:05,440 --> 00:12:14,880
So we're just doing the dry run of super manually without any layers of code being written just to see how this would actually go and work when we actually had a real product out there.

127
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:23,920
So with all that being said, then I started to pitch investors and through that process we landed our first term sheet of around 500 K.

128
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:30,320
And then once we had that term sheet, we also applied around the same time to YC because we had that term sheet.

129
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:33,360
We had them accelerate our interview process.

130
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:35,840
So we had the term sheet in hand.

131
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:37,200
We also got into YC..

132
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:42,080
So at that point, Ilya quit his job and we're both in it full time.

133
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:50,000
Also, and so since you went through YC, you both moved to the Valley for what, three months to go through the program?

134
00:12:50,720 --> 00:12:57,040
We would have if it hadn't been that this is summer 2021 and COVID, so we actually built it out of New York.

135
00:12:57,440 --> 00:13:08,400
I have some slight regrets that that was the case and we didn't get to do that, although we do go there every few months or so to see our advisors there and to like, a lot of our investors are based there.

136
00:13:08,640 --> 00:13:12,320
So it's good to go back, but yeah, we didn't have that in person experience.

137
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:16,400
So in a way, we, we, yeah, we're we didn't have any distractions.

138
00:13:16,560 --> 00:13:22,320
We're just in a tiny office in New York, just working 14 hours a day to make as much progress as possible.

139
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:26,160
When did the product actually launch during YC?

140
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:30,080
Yeah, we launched it in the first couple of weeks of YC.

141
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:31,200
How did that go?

142
00:13:31,440 --> 00:13:33,840
I know you were charging from the very beginning, right?

143
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:47,280
Which was maybe a little counterintuitive compared to what, you know, a lot of venture backed companies would do, especially at the time in 2021 when money was free and there was still a little bit of a just like build it, grow it fast kind of mentality.

144
00:13:48,480 --> 00:13:48,560
Yeah.

145
00:13:48,720 --> 00:14:12,160
Yeah, the biggest thing here in the way I think about charging is that especially in the early days, you really want to figure out who is your customer, who are you building for because the tool that you build, although it has a value proposition, right, that could be pretty broad, there will be an ICP that will be your initial target audience that you need to hyper focus on and make the product better for them before you can think about multiple ICPs.

146
00:14:12,880 --> 00:14:18,000
And so charging for the product is a way to already figure out who that ICP is.

147
00:14:18,640 --> 00:14:30,720
And this way you don't get feedback or worry and get distracted about feedback or usage patterns or issues with people who are just never going to be RSTP, but you can really figure out who has the willingness to pay.

148
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:41,120
If they have the willingness to pay that generally correlates with, okay, this problem clearly resonates with this person, let's then narrow down and focus on making that person's experiences as great as possible.

149
00:14:41,840 --> 00:14:42,960
It makes a ton of sense.

150
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:46,240
Not everybody does that, but it really does make a ton of sense.

151
00:14:46,800 --> 00:14:47,920
How did you structure that?

152
00:14:48,080 --> 00:14:51,280
Was it just a standard seven-day free trial to a hard paywall?

153
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:57,360
Did you have any kind of like free tier or any kind of free stuff that people could continue doing in the app?

154
00:14:57,600 --> 00:15:07,520
Or was it just pay or leave the app at the time?, we had a very simple, I believe, $10 a month that was just the standard paywall.

155
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:14,800
We always had a one week free trial because we believe that people do want to need to experience a product before they can commit.

156
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:25,600
We were not trying to, you know, stick them with a product and a payment that they regretted, but having again that free trial was still a much stronger signal than just totally ungated.

157
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:33,360
And then, yeah, we were not especially in the YC days, we're not trying to revolutionize, you know, the business model.

158
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:36,560
Let's just do the tried and true, just like one week.

159
00:15:36,560 --> 00:15:49,920
One week trial, simple, ten dollars a month so that we can focus our energy on actually building a good product experience, building a good onboarding, building the core product instead of just having to do like a ton of experimentation on the paywall.

160
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:51,200
Obviously that came later.

161
00:15:51,440 --> 00:16:00,800
We've done a lot of variants on the paywall, on the pricing, packaging, and today we actually lead with an annual subscription of around $150 a year.

162
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:06,560
And then we also offer a monthly as a fallback option for people who prefer that.

163
00:16:07,040 --> 00:16:12,880
And we also have a higher tier that comes with a few additional features like AI chat, which comes at $200 a year.

164
00:16:13,760 --> 00:16:19,600
So we have certainly evolved it, but I still think my philosophy is to optimize the paywall.

165
00:16:19,760 --> 00:16:32,480
It's important, but don't over optimize the paywall compared to the actual product value that you're delivering to users, which is the onboarding, is that storytelling and that prepping the user for a successful experience.

166
00:16:32,560 --> 00:16:35,680
And then obviously the core day one plus product experience.

167
00:16:36,320 --> 00:16:37,680
Yeah, I love that perspective.

168
00:16:38,240 --> 00:16:44,560
And the way you put it in the notes was optimize product value, not just the paywall and onboarding.

169
00:16:44,960 --> 00:16:46,640
I kind of harp on it a lot in this podcast.

170
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:48,320
So I get to get on my soapbox again.

171
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:50,080
because you put it so eloquently.

172
00:16:50,880 --> 00:17:08,320
But if you're if you're spending so much time optimizing the onboarding and the paywall and you're getting a ton of trials, but you have a massive trial cancellation rate, and if you think that another two pages in onboarding, another hack on your paywall is what's going to get those trial conversions, that's not how it works.

173
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:12,320
It's because people actually get into the product and they're not finding value.

174
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:21,840
That's a massive optimization lever on free trial conversions that not a lot of people are really spending enough time thinking about after the paywall.

175
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,960
So I love that you brought that up and that's how you think about it it.

176
00:17:25,280 --> 00:17:25,360
Yeah.

177
00:17:25,600 --> 00:17:32,080
I mean, look, all these hacks of like, oh, just one copy change and it drives twenty percent more people.

178
00:17:32,560 --> 00:17:38,560
Like, they're so tempting because you're like, oh, I'm just one iteration away from unlocking this huge product value.

179
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:40,960
And of course, again, I'm not saying do no testing at all.

180
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:51,440
It is an important product surface area, but I think people have taken it maybe to an extreme where they're putting so much effort in that as opposed to, again, working on the core product experience.

181
00:17:51,920 --> 00:17:57,600
Another really interesting thing that we found in some of those experiments is that the initial metrics actually look really promising.

182
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,920
For example, the STAR trial rate does increase with a few changes that you might make.

183
00:18:02,240 --> 00:18:04,400
And I can give specific examples if that's helpful.

184
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:11,680
But at the end of the day, when you actually look, who converted, did those payments go through or did they fail?

185
00:18:11,840 --> 00:18:13,120
Did people ask for refunds?

186
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:24,480
The delta after all that work with the bottom line is actually, we found for a lot of these experiments, not that big, if not sometimes even negative, even though the headline number of trial start rate is higher.

187
00:18:25,280 --> 00:18:35,040
So I've kind of gotten a little, yeah, disillusioned with these like, again, almost get rich quick schemes that feels like paywall optimization is becoming.

188
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:40,000
And it's like at the end of the day, it just make a better product for people and that's how you drive more revenue.

189
00:18:40,400 --> 00:18:40,480
Yeah.

190
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:44,640
And to be clear, I mean, some of those hacks work and you're experimenting with them, you try stuff.

191
00:18:45,120 --> 00:19:02,400
But I love, and I'm just going to read it from my notes because I love the way you framed this is that you're the ultimate judge, the ultimate metric you should be measuring against is not trial starts and some of those early funnel, not even like initial conversions and stuff, but it's net revenue after refunds per exposed user.

192
00:19:02,800 --> 00:19:05,680
And then here's the kicker on a matured cohort.

193
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:12,880
How did you decide on that specifically as your gold standard metric to measure any experiment against?

194
00:19:13,760 --> 00:19:20,160
It came from the experience of running a lot of these and implementing some changes that looked really good.

195
00:19:20,400 --> 00:19:26,320
And in hindsight, again, at that time, maybe we just didn't know that we had to wait and see all these other metrics mature.

196
00:19:26,800 --> 00:19:28,160
And also we didn't have instrumentation.

197
00:19:28,320 --> 00:19:31,760
It was really easy day one to see start trial rate, right?

198
00:19:32,080 --> 00:19:33,680
And that's where you get the most data.

199
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:42,400
That's where you're most likely to get a p value that is statistically significant because you get so many people at that level.

200
00:19:43,040 --> 00:19:51,040
But what we learned and started to implement soon after we started to really iterate on the paywall is that's not what actually matters.

201
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:58,800
You have to actually look at the metrics of actual subscription conversion, which again, that's pretty well known thing.

202
00:19:58,960 --> 00:20:10,560
I don't think that's new, but the more important thing there is that some of those subscriptions, even though initially they convert, the payment doesn't actually go through, and a lot of the refund rate can be a huge delta.

203
00:20:11,040 --> 00:20:24,720
So what I found is that anything that kind of forces or tricks people into starting a trial, you'll pay it when it comes to refunds, and that is a worse experience because those people who ask for refunds are generally pissed.

204
00:20:25,280 --> 00:20:27,200
They're not happy that they have to go through that process.

205
00:20:27,600 --> 00:20:32,960
You're also creating this negative user sentiment around the product that you don't want.

206
00:20:33,200 --> 00:20:39,360
And then, of course, the matured cohort piece is important because people won't ask for the refund necessarily on day one.

207
00:20:39,520 --> 00:20:45,200
They'll ask for the refund after they've actually seen that the payment hit their bank account.

208
00:20:45,680 --> 00:20:55,360
So you have to think about, okay, trials start on this date, give it an extra week for them to convert, give it at least two extra weeks for the dust to settle from refunds.

209
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:00,880
So actually, again, it's very tempting to make a quick decision on trials, but you have to give it more time to settle.

210
00:21:00,880 --> 00:21:02,800
to give it more time to see the whole story play out.

211
00:21:03,120 --> 00:21:07,440
Yeah, it makes so much sense, but it's hard to do and it's hard to find the discipline.

212
00:21:07,680 --> 00:21:21,760
And then, you know, like you were saying, the tooling, I mean, revenue cap, we just, you know, in the last year or two, finally expanded the experimentation window where you can actually go back and look at three months and six months.

213
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:25,200
And I think we, now it's like over a year that you can look back.

214
00:21:26,080 --> 00:21:37,680
And, and, you know, we've tried to make that really easy because it's one of those things, like in, if you're running your own analytics, like it's just hard to do that and then have the discipline to go back and check it and all that kind of stuff.

215
00:21:38,080 --> 00:21:41,440
It's all these things that make sense, but then you actually have to execute.

216
00:21:41,680 --> 00:21:48,800
You actually have to do it and then have the discipline to keep looking back and maybe reversing some of those decisions that you were really excited about.

217
00:21:48,960 --> 00:22:00,400
Like, I mean, because I would imagine you do sometimes choose a winner on some of those early metrics, then you don't see till three months later that it's, that it's something you actually wanted to reverse.

218
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:03,680
Has that happened frequently or how does that work for you?

219
00:22:04,160 --> 00:22:12,720
Yeah, I don't think we've implemented a decision that we've had to reverse, but yeah, there have been many things where we've been really excited.

220
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:14,240
about the initial result.

221
00:22:14,880 --> 00:22:18,560
And the hardest thing is not necessarily the discipline and checking back in the data.

222
00:22:18,720 --> 00:22:20,320
It's the fact that it takes just way longer.

223
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:22,080
And you have data you're excited about.

224
00:22:22,240 --> 00:22:23,360
You want to be able to action it.

225
00:22:23,760 --> 00:22:40,640
And the idea of just sitting there on something that could be really promising for an extra month or depending on X number of months, depending on how much, again, users you need to validate and get to statistical significance, that part is hard of like just sitting on that and being patient.

226
00:22:41,040 --> 00:22:43,040
And it also means you can iterate as fast, right?

227
00:22:43,280 --> 00:22:44,880
You can implement those learnings as fast.

228
00:22:45,040 --> 00:22:45,680
So I get it.

229
00:22:45,920 --> 00:22:47,360
And I was that impatient person.

230
00:22:47,680 --> 00:22:50,960
What we found is, for example, I'll give you two examples actually.

231
00:22:51,360 --> 00:22:58,480
When it comes to the paywall, we tested a hard paywall where we basically removed the X dismiss button.

232
00:22:58,880 --> 00:23:01,360
So people had to start a trial in order to proceed.

233
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:15,040
And then we also tested a version where there was still a dismiss button on the main paywall, but then if you dismissed it, we gave people an offer of X percent off that they couldn't X out of.

234
00:23:15,200 --> 00:23:16,480
So they would have to start a trial.

235
00:23:16,720 --> 00:23:20,160
If they hadn't started at the baseline price, they would have to start at the lower price.

236
00:23:20,400 --> 00:23:21,360
Again, it's a trial.

237
00:23:21,520 --> 00:23:22,400
It's not a payment.

238
00:23:23,040 --> 00:23:24,480
So they still cancel it.

239
00:23:25,120 --> 00:23:30,160
But both of those look so promising on the trial numbers and it makes sense, right?

240
00:23:30,400 --> 00:23:32,720
People have already put in all that time in the onboarding flow.

241
00:23:32,880 --> 00:23:34,720
They actually want to see what this app is all about.

242
00:23:35,360 --> 00:23:36,960
And we're like, great, this is great.

243
00:23:37,200 --> 00:23:38,160
Why are we sitting on this?

244
00:23:38,320 --> 00:23:41,120
Why, why, why shouldn't we just put this in front of everyone?

245
00:23:41,840 --> 00:23:48,720
And the answer came down to the refunds and the refunds spiked by way more than the gain in Star trials.

246
00:23:49,440 --> 00:23:56,160
And again, it took us a while to get to that data, but once that data came in, I think it was very clear that that was not the right thing to do.

247
00:23:56,960 --> 00:24:00,720
So again, I feel like I've been a broken record already about this.

248
00:24:01,760 --> 00:24:10,000
But I think my take away from this is that just the mechanical clever things don't actually work and you'll pay the price for those things.

249
00:24:10,320 --> 00:24:16,640
And the only thing that actually works is once you have something that generally makes sense when it comes to the paywalls, just work on the product.

250
00:24:17,760 --> 00:24:18,960
So how does it work now?

251
00:24:19,920 --> 00:24:26,080
You said you tested a fully hard paywall and they tested the X to a fully hard paywall with a discount.

252
00:24:26,720 --> 00:24:29,280
What happens now if they X out of the paywall?

253
00:24:29,440 --> 00:24:32,400
Because you said that you don't really have a freemium experience, right?

254
00:24:32,960 --> 00:24:34,880
We have a very limited freemium experience.

255
00:24:34,960 --> 00:24:36,960
So if you X out of the paywall, you take your paywall.

256
00:24:36,960 --> 00:24:38,160
You're taken to the speech assessment.

257
00:24:38,400 --> 00:24:39,360
You'll be able to speak.

258
00:24:39,840 --> 00:24:42,480
You'll still see the assessment results.

259
00:24:42,640 --> 00:24:44,240
We'll break down all the things that we heard.

260
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:46,320
It's already a pretty valuable experience.

261
00:24:46,480 --> 00:24:48,800
Like some people work with coaches to just get that assessment.

262
00:24:48,960 --> 00:24:50,000
So we give that for free.

263
00:24:50,480 --> 00:24:57,680
And then you can also access the rest of the day one experience and do a practice set and I believe also coach video for free.

264
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:02,320
And then only on day two you can start a trial at that point if you wish to continue.

265
00:25:03,120 --> 00:25:10,720
So we've kind of, yeah, found the middle ground and let's give some of the product experience, but we also have an experience where we have daily plans.

266
00:25:11,040 --> 00:25:14,480
So it's easy to say, okay, the first daily plan for free on us.

267
00:25:15,120 --> 00:25:20,480
After that, again, you don't have to pay right away, but you have to start a seven day trial to continue.

268
00:25:21,040 --> 00:25:29,680
It's essentially a reverse trial at that point because you are like giving value, but only for that first set of lessons and the speech assessment.

269
00:25:30,240 --> 00:25:33,440
These are the kind of things I think more people should be experimenting with.

270
00:25:34,640 --> 00:25:44,320
Is there something, is there a great experience I can give to folks that helps them see the value of the product, but that there's some kind of limit to it?

271
00:25:45,040 --> 00:25:47,520
And a great example, I just brought this up on a live stream yesterday.

272
00:25:47,680 --> 00:25:57,680
I've been looking at trading card games apps a lot in the past few weeks because we're going to do a live app valuation on Subclub of one of these trading card game apps.

273
00:25:57,760 --> 00:26:02,160
And it's like a card scanner to see the value of your Pokemon cards and Magic cards and things like that.

274
00:26:02,720 --> 00:26:14,080
And one of the really cool things they did, and I hadn't seen this exact user experience before, but what they do is they give essentially the full value of the product, but then they limit it to a certain number of scans.

275
00:26:14,320 --> 00:26:15,600
And then they make it really clear.

276
00:26:15,920 --> 00:26:19,360
So you've got your whole scanner UI where you're scanning your Pokemon card.

277
00:26:19,920 --> 00:26:22,800
And it says like thirty five scans left.

278
00:26:23,040 --> 00:26:28,160
And then 34 scans left and then you scan one, 33 scans left.

279
00:26:28,560 --> 00:26:37,200
And so it's really preparing you for that moment that the paywall comes, but it's giving you the full value of the product and a really fantastic experience.

280
00:26:37,360 --> 00:26:41,520
Generally, I mean, some of them are really bad at scanning the cards, as I've learned.

281
00:26:42,080 --> 00:26:45,760
My kids are into both Magic and Pokemon and they've been pulling me in recently.

282
00:26:46,080 --> 00:26:50,000
So I've been actually using them with their card collections and I've collected a few now.

283
00:26:50,320 --> 00:26:52,080
So some of them actually are a bad experience.

284
00:26:52,480 --> 00:27:01,360
And this is kind of your point, those like those apps that are a fantastic experience, it's way better for me to have that experience and then use the app product.

285
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
And then I'm going to be a product user for life.

286
00:27:04,240 --> 00:27:09,440
I'm going to be, you know, actually subscribe versus if I'd done a seven day free trial, it looks really great.

287
00:27:09,680 --> 00:27:13,200
I started the trial, but then I immediately canceled because the product's not delivering.

288
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:33,200
So if you can actually deliver value and people actually care, that kind of finding a way to do a reverse trial, finding a way to do some kind of limited experience, trying to just find ways to like give value really preps the customer for that next paywall that they're going to be way more likely to convert.

289
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:40,640
And the cool thing is that at least for us, you don't need need to do that at the expense of that paywall and onboarding.

290
00:27:40,960 --> 00:27:47,920
There will be some people who through the onboarding experience have seen enough about the product that they're prepared to start a free seven day trial.

291
00:27:48,400 --> 00:28:01,440
There will be some people who are more skeptical and maybe they have been burned in the past by free trials or other apps that have shady product experiences and they even just putting their credit card down, they don't want to do it even if it's for a free experience.

292
00:28:02,080 --> 00:28:03,520
So for those people, you can offer that.

293
00:28:03,840 --> 00:28:17,440
I think offering for everyone and just removing the free trial paywall from the experience, I think that could be tricky because you can also maybe risk that people think, Oh, this is just a free product and then get kind of upset when you show them a paywall.

294
00:28:17,600 --> 00:28:29,600
So I think we've kind of found an interesting, nice middle ground where we do show them that paywall, but they can get out of it and they can get a little bit of a free taster before they commit to putting their credit card down for that free experience.

295
00:28:30,080 --> 00:28:37,360
Do you think you'll ever have a more kind of premium tier where people can get some value and continue using the app?

296
00:28:37,680 --> 00:28:39,120
Or like, what's your philosophy on that?

297
00:28:39,280 --> 00:28:47,840
Because I know when we were preparing for the episode, you mentioned that you really enjoyed the Jim Kansu, the Chief Product Officer at Duolingo, his episode.

298
00:28:47,920 --> 00:28:54,880
And like their big thesis at Duolingo is that that freemium base is the growth engine that they leverage.

299
00:28:55,520 --> 00:28:56,640
What's your philosophy on that?

300
00:28:56,880 --> 00:29:03,920
Do you ever see freemium or do you think that this form of reverse trial and giving away value is enough to keep propelling the business forward?

301
00:29:04,480 --> 00:29:04,960
Jim is great.

302
00:29:05,280 --> 00:29:08,000
I'm a big fan of him and the work he's done at Duolingo.

303
00:29:08,240 --> 00:29:13,040
I will say though that it depends on the it depends on the product and it depends on the business model.

304
00:29:13,600 --> 00:29:24,640
And I think the worst thing that someone can do is like take some playbooks and some elements of a playbook from one app, combine it with a playbook of a different app and create this mish mash strategy, Duolingo strategy.

305
00:29:25,280 --> 00:29:30,240
has always been huge freemium base, right, that they monetize a portion of.

306
00:29:30,400 --> 00:29:35,440
And because they have this, like, all the languages, all the levels, they're teaching the basics of everything.

307
00:29:35,760 --> 00:29:39,040
They now have all these new skills, give this product away for free.

308
00:29:39,200 --> 00:29:41,280
Even if you monetize a percentage of it, you're fine.

309
00:29:41,920 --> 00:29:48,080
Our philosophy from the beginning is we're not going after this, like, huge base of learners who are at basic level.

310
00:29:48,400 --> 00:29:50,160
We are actually intentionally ignoring them.

311
00:29:50,320 --> 00:29:52,160
We're not teaching the basics of English.

312
00:29:52,400 --> 00:29:53,680
We're not teaching words.

313
00:29:53,760 --> 00:30:00,480
We're focusing on a very different audience that is actually somewhat ignored or deprioritized by a lot of these apps, which is people.

314
00:30:00,480 --> 00:30:04,560
which are people who already have a pretty good command of the language and want to take it to the next level.

315
00:30:05,200 --> 00:30:11,600
So people are in intermediate to advanced level and for whom English is important enough that they want to put more effort behind it.

316
00:30:11,760 --> 00:30:14,000
And those people are generally career professionals.

317
00:30:14,480 --> 00:30:15,520
They care more about English.

318
00:30:15,680 --> 00:30:16,480
They're willing to pay.

319
00:30:16,800 --> 00:30:25,200
The alternative to what we offer is a way more expensive one-on-one speech coaching experience with an English teacher or an accent coach.

320
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:36,880
So it's a totally different audience where the intent is much higher and the ROI is also way higher than as a hobby learning the basics of Spanish or Japanese or something like that.

321
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:43,200
So totally different ICP, different value proposition, different willingness to pay.

322
00:30:43,920 --> 00:30:47,440
And so our entire business has been optimized around that.

323
00:30:47,840 --> 00:30:56,640
And it makes sense that again for Duolingo, if they do way too heavy monetization at the sacrifice of their free base, that's the free base is what got them here.

324
00:30:56,800 --> 00:30:57,760
So that doesn't make sense.

325
00:30:57,840 --> 00:31:00,240
Whereas for us, that was never the case.

326
00:31:00,480 --> 00:31:06,080
We're always hyper focused on this person who has a willingness to pay if they get a product that solves that need.

327
00:31:06,800 --> 00:31:14,720
And if obviously the cost of that product is a significant reduction to working one on one with the coach, which is the case for us.

328
00:31:15,440 --> 00:31:29,600
As you say that, though, it does make me think that at some point, you know, as you grow and have, you know, more time and resources to experiment with, then maybe there is a cheaper tier that is aimed at the more casual but still willing to pay.

329
00:31:29,920 --> 00:31:42,080
And again, I think this is something if you're building an app and you start to see, because I imagine this wasn't like from the get-go, you realize, oh, we're going to sell this to professionals who are willing to pay hundreds of dollars for this.

330
00:31:42,880 --> 00:31:51,680
So if you start seeing yourself pulled in that direction, like embrace it because it can like, you know, $150 a year and then a premium tier above that, that's $200.

331
00:31:51,840 --> 00:31:55,600
And honestly, like you're probably just scratching the surface of what you could do.

332
00:31:56,000 --> 00:32:02,800
And then you mentioned you even have one on one coaching that, you know, it's not a huge part of the business, but it's like $400 or $500 an hour.

333
00:32:03,200 --> 00:32:07,200
So there is like massive willingness to pay when it's on that side of things.

334
00:32:07,440 --> 00:32:17,920
And so by not chasing that consumer and lower willingness to pay market, you're building a really fantastic business on that higher ROI, higher intent, more disposable income and all those kinds of things.

335
00:32:18,400 --> 00:32:18,480
Yeah.

336
00:32:18,720 --> 00:32:20,560
And that served us well, to this point.

337
00:32:20,720 --> 00:32:23,600
And I I'm not going to say we'll never deviate from that strategy.

338
00:32:23,680 --> 00:32:24,720
I think for sure there will be.

339
00:32:26,000 --> 00:32:36,160
experiments with the packaging, especially as we go right now, we're still primarily serving people in the US who are immigrants and who also have that higher ability to pay.

340
00:32:36,640 --> 00:32:42,240
Whereas outside of the US, spoken English fluency is sometimes as important, right?

341
00:32:42,480 --> 00:32:49,680
Because you can get a higher paid job, you can get better opportunities, you can go to study at a different international university and so forth.

342
00:32:49,760 --> 00:32:57,600
It unlocks even in some cases a bigger delta between the opportunities you could get without that clear English and those you can get with clear communication.

343
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:00,640
But of course, the basic ability to pay in general.

344
00:33:02,240 --> 00:33:08,720
And I think as we do more of the international strategy, we certainly can revisit and should revisit like the localization and things like that.

345
00:33:09,360 --> 00:33:32,000
So I'm not saying this is a strategy necessarily that we will rigidly keep as we continue to scale, but certainly the principle that we're serving a different learner level where they are higher intent and willing to pay for a product that solves their needs instead of just like a hobby learner, I think that will most likely continue to be the case.

346
00:33:32,560 --> 00:33:34,320
How did you figure out that that was your market?

347
00:33:36,400 --> 00:33:37,200
We created this product.

348
00:33:37,600 --> 00:33:42,400
I went back to the basics and I said, well, I was an international student when I first experienced this problem.

349
00:33:43,040 --> 00:33:45,920
And it's like such a, almost a cliché playbook, right?

350
00:33:46,160 --> 00:33:49,600
Let's just put this in colleges because college students are great adapters.

351
00:33:49,840 --> 00:34:04,000
And again, this is what happens going back to my point about, like, you take a playbook from a totally different product, like dating app or something, where they all start college campus or Facebook famously or whatever, and you apply it to a product where the ICP and the value prop is super different.

352
00:34:04,160 --> 00:34:05,360
And of course, it's not going to work.

353
00:34:05,680 --> 00:34:10,560
So what we found in that process is that it's not like international students were a terrible audience for us.

354
00:34:10,720 --> 00:34:12,560
I mean, we still do get a lot of students in the app.

355
00:34:13,360 --> 00:34:24,160
But we clearly realize that A, they are a bit younger and they have more, they still have some plasticity and they're still surrounded by native speakers in very social environments.

356
00:34:24,640 --> 00:34:31,440
So their rate of improvement naturally will be higher without needing this additional resource if they just focus on it themselves.

357
00:34:31,920 --> 00:34:37,040
The second thing is the willingness to pay and the ability to pay for college students is of course lower.

358
00:34:37,600 --> 00:35:00,960
And also just the, the, the fact that they maybe have not seen the price of their communication as much because they're not yet in a meeting where people will overlook them and listen to the native speaker over them or they're they haven't yet been in a job interview where they realized that the reason that that didn't work out was their communication and all of those things.

359
00:35:01,440 --> 00:35:08,720
So both the willingness to pay and the realization of how much it impacts your income and your career advancement has not happened yet.

360
00:35:09,200 --> 00:35:19,040
So we found that actually the audience that resonates best with this were people who were in the workplace already and who were already seeing the cost and therefore the ROI of investing their career.

361
00:35:19,120 --> 00:35:23,360
And of course they were making having actually income that they could put towards this.

362
00:35:24,000 --> 00:35:30,400
And has there been any kind of more direct B2B play with companies?

363
00:35:31,600 --> 00:35:42,800
Because I did see on Hacker News, the founder of Expo said that they talked about having an employee expense this as a solution to help them improve their job performance.

364
00:35:43,360 --> 00:35:49,920
Yeah, we've seen that quite a bit with just inbound companies will reach to us to make this happen for their employees.

365
00:35:50,160 --> 00:35:56,240
Or we're also seeing that people will, you know, buy this and then have the company pay for it, which is great.

366
00:35:56,720 --> 00:36:02,160
We haven't pursued it as much as a intentional go to market purely because of resourcing.

367
00:36:02,480 --> 00:36:10,720
We're still a very small team and we've gotten to this point by being relentlessly focused on the consumer experience.

368
00:36:11,280 --> 00:36:20,880
And I think the muscle of building a B2B company or B2B go to market is pretty different and I think it will make sense for us at a future stage potentially.

369
00:36:21,280 --> 00:36:26,480
But for now, the consumer market is so big that we've just let it be our main intentional focus.

370
00:36:26,640 --> 00:36:34,720
And then we get this nice additional effect of inbound from companies because they know that the consumer product is so loved.

371
00:36:35,200 --> 00:36:35,280
Yeah.

372
00:36:36,160 --> 00:36:43,040
One of the things you touched on there, and we've kind of been touching on throughout the entire conversation is user obsession and focus.

373
00:36:43,680 --> 00:36:47,440
I love your approach to understanding users.

374
00:36:47,680 --> 00:36:57,360
So why don't you take me through the kind of layers of how you speak to your users, how you interact with users, and how you let that impact the direction of the product.

375
00:36:58,080 --> 00:37:11,280
So we've put a bunch of things in place to make the user obsession not just something we talk about, but like a muscle that we keep fresh, because I think the user base is always also going to change and their expectations are going to change.

376
00:37:11,680 --> 00:37:14,640
You're going to bring in naturally newer people as you expand.

377
00:37:14,800 --> 00:37:21,200
So it's really important not just to have this, do a bunch of user interviews at the beginning and then be like, okay, that's our user.

378
00:37:21,280 --> 00:37:23,680
And then just like not evolve that understanding.

379
00:37:24,480 --> 00:37:32,640
So the things that we've implemented is, for example, we send out automated invites to users and we pick a variety of different people.

380
00:37:33,200 --> 00:37:35,680
Sometimes it's subscribers who have been with us for two or three years.

381
00:37:35,920 --> 00:37:40,800
Sometimes people who just started a trial a week ago and converted or didn't convert.

382
00:37:41,200 --> 00:37:44,720
And we ask them to jump on a call with our team for 15 minutes and share their feedback.

383
00:37:45,040 --> 00:37:49,760
And that invitation automatically invites the people on the team who have availability in their calendar.

384
00:37:50,000 --> 00:38:00,800
So what that creates is that basically every single person on the team, myself, my co-founder, our engineers, our growth people, everyone is in at least two user interviews a week.

385
00:38:01,280 --> 00:38:06,000
So we're actively talking to people for at least 15 minutes, like twice a week.

386
00:38:06,640 --> 00:38:07,680
And that's just one example.

387
00:38:08,080 --> 00:38:13,920
Another example is we've created this super user group and we hold weekly or so calls with them.

388
00:38:14,080 --> 00:38:21,520
And that's a great opportunity for this is the people who already engage with the app a lot to figure out how do we make these experiences better for them?

389
00:38:21,920 --> 00:38:40,640
And where that's been really impactful is to actually show to have our engineers demo new features that we're working on and get feedback from those users on those features so that they're a part of the product evolution and decision making before the feature is even out to users.

390
00:38:40,960 --> 00:38:44,800
So it's been really powerful to figure out what is making sense, what's not making sense.

391
00:38:45,360 --> 00:38:52,800
One thing about our product is that it's deceptively very complicated when it comes to pronunciation, speech.

392
00:38:53,360 --> 00:38:56,720
It's not like teaching grammar where there are correct and incorrect rules.

393
00:38:56,960 --> 00:39:44,800
There's just so many elements and details and exceptions and nuances and it can get really complicated to build which is what we want like a really user friendly product experience while also not making it over complicated and intimidating to use and understand so it's really important to get our real users eyes on features to validate is this actually making sense before we ship it so those are just two quick examples but sometimes we also bring people in office and we watch how they actually use the product and we watch what they tap like what they're spending most time on of course we also have analytics around all of this that we also watch but being able to actually verbally talk to users and actually see them interact with the product, I think is an underrated skill.

394
00:39:45,360 --> 00:39:55,440
So building these, again, automated ways for us, semi-automated ways, of course, then we actually are in these calls, but semi-automated ways to have more user face time has been super helpful.

395
00:39:55,920 --> 00:39:58,240
I love that level of customer obsession.

396
00:39:58,560 --> 00:39:59,920
And it speaks to being such a small team.

397
00:40:00,080 --> 00:40:00,800
And we'll get to this later.

398
00:40:00,960 --> 00:40:10,320
You're only ten people and at ten million dollars in ARR, which is incredible, a million dollars per employee as a small scrappy company is incredible.

399
00:40:10,560 --> 00:40:15,760
But that user obsession, you know, you're meetinging with users two a week, everybody at the company.

400
00:40:15,920 --> 00:40:17,840
So that's 20 calls a week at least.

401
00:40:18,560 --> 00:40:22,480
You have a super user group and then you bring people into the office.

402
00:40:22,640 --> 00:40:32,560
There's like even layers to it, but that qualitative feedback and seeing people actually struggle is so different than the quantitative, like just look at the analytics.

403
00:40:32,880 --> 00:40:43,040
And then when you see the analytics and you're like, oh, it looks like something's going on here, then you actually can verify that with the super user group or with these in office testing and those kind of things.

404
00:40:43,280 --> 00:40:46,320
So I love that kind of multi-layer approach.

405
00:40:46,480 --> 00:40:55,440
And then you're also not over indexing on the super users because you're having meetings with the random folks and then you're actually having people in office to get like the human touch.

406
00:40:55,680 --> 00:41:15,200
So I absolutely love how you've built that and structured it to kind of touch all those different points and not kind of because it is a bit of a trap sometimes where if you only did the super user group, you'd probably be pushing the product in directions that work for them but not some of the more casual users.

407
00:41:15,600 --> 00:41:21,280
And then if you only did the kind of casual user sampling, you might miss some of the things that the super users care about.

408
00:41:21,360 --> 00:41:32,400
And so by layering it all like that, you're really getting the kind of kind of breadth of qualitative feedback that you just never would get in analytics and never would get if you didn't do it in this multilayer strategy.

409
00:41:32,800 --> 00:41:36,720
Did that just come organically or how did you decide on that approach?

410
00:41:37,200 --> 00:41:40,080
I mean, it's evolved over the years, for sure.

411
00:41:40,480 --> 00:41:45,360
And more recently, again, we have been figuring out ways to build more and more automation.

412
00:41:45,760 --> 00:41:47,600
All of us here in the company use cloud a lot.

413
00:41:47,760 --> 00:41:59,360
So we're figuring out how we do these things and so that we don't have to, because if you have to have a person email manually, you know, a bunch of users and that's great, one off, but it's a one off.

414
00:42:00,080 --> 00:42:07,440
And so these automations help us, again, make sure that we maintain that muscle instead of just making it a one-off effort.

415
00:42:07,920 --> 00:42:23,040
But I will say, though, to your point about the diversity of perspectives, one really important flag is that you're always going to get more feedback and you're going to get more people to jump on calls who are happy users because they're like, oh my gosh, my favorite product reached out to me for my feedback.

416
00:42:23,120 --> 00:42:24,000
I want to book this call.

417
00:42:24,640 --> 00:42:28,640
And people who didn't have a good experience are going to be less likely to jump on those calls.

418
00:42:28,880 --> 00:42:36,160
So it's really important to keep that in mind and to be aware of the bias in the feedback, in the features that people are also asking for.

419
00:42:36,960 --> 00:43:02,240
So a lot of times people who did not have a good experience or did not feel like the product met what they were looking for, those people might not want to jump on a call, but if we give them just like a three question mini survey in an email, but they don't even have to click on anything, they can just like respond in an email, they'll be more likely to answer that or when someone goes to cancel their subscription or their trial, having like a mini flow there that asks them what actually went wrong.

420
00:43:02,960 --> 00:43:15,280
So yeah, I think just being aware of that bias and making sure that you're not overly indexing on like making those happy people happier, which is important, but that could also lead you toward like making this like super niche, super technical product.

421
00:43:15,920 --> 00:43:21,600
And you also want to create a product that has, especially as it grows, wider and wider appeal.

422
00:43:22,320 --> 00:43:22,960
Such great advice.

423
00:43:23,200 --> 00:43:24,800
Gosh, there's so many things I want to talk about.

424
00:43:24,880 --> 00:43:37,120
We don't have a ton of time left, but maybe we can kind of speedrun some of the virality and kind of growth things you've done because you've gotten some really cool growth wins and some viral moments and things like that.

425
00:43:37,520 --> 00:43:44,240
What would you say were the kind of maybe top three most impactful lessons on the growth side or top three kind of viral moments?

426
00:43:44,560 --> 00:43:48,000
Well, for sure, the top one was the X and Oracle that we built.

427
00:43:48,160 --> 00:44:01,440
So yeah, our team had this idea that if we could build a tool that could predict where a person was from just based on ten seconds of them speaking, it could have this kind of viral potential.

428
00:44:02,080 --> 00:44:05,920
And so we built and trained a model in house to do exactly that.

429
00:44:06,240 --> 00:44:18,880
So I think this also speaks to, again, a really important part of our success is that we have an AudioML team in house building these and training these really differentiated models from scratch with our own data.

430
00:44:19,360 --> 00:44:27,120
And we also have the expert speech coaches who are a huge part of how we label the transcribe and just train these models to be better.

431
00:44:27,360 --> 00:44:29,680
So that's a really big differentiation in our product.

432
00:44:30,000 --> 00:44:34,400
And the accent oracle was built based on one of those in-house created models.

433
00:44:34,960 --> 00:44:37,280
And it worked.

434
00:44:38,000 --> 00:44:40,480
It was the most magical thing I think we've done.

435
00:44:40,960 --> 00:44:47,840
And the best part about it is that the way that it spread is that every month I write the investor updates.

436
00:44:48,000 --> 00:44:49,040
I've been doing for five years.

437
00:44:49,600 --> 00:44:55,200
That month that the accent oracle was built, I sent it out, I think, even day after or so to our investors.

438
00:44:55,440 --> 00:44:57,200
One of them was an angel investor.

439
00:44:57,680 --> 00:44:58,320
It was Korean.

440
00:44:58,720 --> 00:44:59,840
And he was like, oh, this is cool.

441
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:03,280
I'll put it on threads and he puts it on threads.

442
00:45:03,440 --> 00:45:08,320
And then I believe the next day we had 100,000 people in Korea try this thing.

443
00:45:08,960 --> 00:45:11,680
It's again, wild and unlike anything we've seen before.

444
00:45:11,840 --> 00:45:14,480
So of course, when that happened, we're like, let's make this better.

445
00:45:15,120 --> 00:45:20,560
Let's invest a lot of time in making the model more accurate, making sure it doesn't crash and all these things.

446
00:45:20,880 --> 00:45:24,080
And it was a really wonderful way to get people familiar with the product.

447
00:45:24,480 --> 00:45:27,440
And we made it like there was no gate.

448
00:45:27,600 --> 00:45:28,480
There was no free trial.

449
00:45:28,560 --> 00:45:29,920
It was like a pretty magical experience.

450
00:45:30,080 --> 00:45:31,680
You don't even have to download the app, by the way.

451
00:45:31,920 --> 00:45:35,680
My co-founder actually made this decision to just put this in a separate web app.

452
00:45:36,320 --> 00:45:42,560
where you just go to accentoracle.com, you record your voice instantly, it spits out your feedback, your results.

453
00:45:42,720 --> 00:45:59,360
So it was optimized for this frictionless viral moment that then exposes people to bold voice as a brand and then invites them into the app to continue discovering more things about their speech and then actually improving and working on their pronunciation and speech as well.

454
00:45:59,840 --> 00:46:06,000
Has that been fairly evergreen or have you taken lessons from that in your ads and other viral content?

455
00:46:06,400 --> 00:46:06,640
For sure.

456
00:46:06,960 --> 00:46:11,680
Yeah, it actually surprised us how much it actually has lasting appeal.

457
00:46:11,840 --> 00:46:16,000
There are people who use it., who keep coming back, it will randomly go viral.

458
00:46:16,160 --> 00:46:19,280
Again, with viral things, what makes them beautiful is also what makes them freaky.

459
00:46:20,000 --> 00:46:25,120
You can't overengineer them like with ads where you know exactly, I'm spending this much, I'm getting this much out of it.

460
00:46:25,360 --> 00:46:28,480
But one thing that has proven true is that when we invest in it.

461
00:46:28,800 --> 00:46:39,200
So for example, we recently added this really cool feature where it not just tells you the country where your accent is from, but it also goes down to the city level.

462
00:46:40,080 --> 00:46:47,680
And once we implemented that, it had another viral boost because again, had this new unique edge to it.

463
00:46:48,240 --> 00:47:07,440
So yeah, we can constantly see it be have this evergreen value with like kind of random unpredictable spikes, but we have a few levers where we can boost it by adding new features or another thing that we did is that we showed people a snippet of the sample of the full results that they would get in the app.

464
00:47:08,000 --> 00:47:12,000
So they're not just seeing this result and then they have to make the leap of faith to go into the app.

465
00:47:12,320 --> 00:47:20,320
We're telling them, by the way, here are some of the other things you'll see in the app and that invites to get to the app or it boosts the conversion rate to get them to the app.

466
00:47:20,720 --> 00:47:21,680
Yeah, that's so cool.

467
00:47:22,000 --> 00:47:25,360
What's the number two top growth hack lesson viral moment.

468
00:47:26,080 --> 00:47:33,760
Probably when it comes to viral things, the second thing that we did, which is again kind of funny and unpredictable, is just like social media and TikTok.

469
00:47:34,320 --> 00:47:41,120
There was this trend that was going around with people crying on TikTok when the app gave them low scores.

470
00:47:41,280 --> 00:47:51,600
And in particular, there was this one creator who got, I don't know, she got like 300 million views in like a few months or something pretty crazy like that.

471
00:47:51,920 --> 00:47:54,240
Again, completely not boosted by ads.

472
00:47:55,040 --> 00:48:04,400
So again, another really cool way of getting the product in a more lighthearted, accessible way into the kind of people's feeds.

473
00:48:04,640 --> 00:48:12,640
So we're not just doing like this, some more serious marketing, but we also have these other lighter ways to get in front of people and make them aware of both voices.

474
00:48:12,960 --> 00:48:33,040
Of course, when you do humorous content, you're going to get huge viral spikes, but they're not going to have the same level of intent that you can get from like, you know, paid ads, for example, or word of mouth, obviously, where you can really target the messaging, but it's still a really nice, I think, machine to have working alongside the paid marketing efforts.

475
00:48:33,280 --> 00:48:37,920
Well, for number three then, I want to ask, what is your kind of bread and butter butter growth tactic?

476
00:48:38,240 --> 00:48:39,520
Do you rely on paid ads?

477
00:48:40,000 --> 00:48:43,520
What's the bulk of what you put your growth effort into?

478
00:48:44,000 --> 00:48:50,240
I think it's a mix, for sure, of product led viral or shareable features plus paid marketing.

479
00:48:50,880 --> 00:48:56,800
On the paid marketing side, I know it's very vilified and a lot of people are against it, and I think that's taken to an extreme.

480
00:48:57,520 --> 00:49:00,960
And realistically, even those people are saying ads are bad are actually running ads.

481
00:49:01,440 --> 00:49:05,360
My experience with it is that there's a good way to run ads and a bad way to run ads.

482
00:49:05,840 --> 00:49:10,240
The good way to do it, I think, is to just be fully aware of your unit economics.

483
00:49:10,800 --> 00:49:12,240
So you're not spending money on.

484
00:49:12,480 --> 00:49:53,680
on again low value customers when it comes to again ltb over caq so there's quite a bit of discipline there that needs to happen to make sure that the unit economics are strong and one really powerful thing that I have found in that process is using payback period to your advantage and making that as short as possible because it gives you upfront a lot of clarity on a user's LTB and what I mean by that is when I already referred to this before with having most users by default being presented with the annual plan as opposed to the monthly plan what that means is that with an annual plan, we get the cash upfront from users after their seven day free trial.

485
00:49:54,000 --> 00:49:59,120
So we know right away from a particular channel it drove this many subscribers, this is how much we spend.

486
00:49:59,440 --> 00:50:12,720
And then we know exactly how effective that marketing was as opposed to monthly where you get the initial monthly subscriber, but you don't actually know will they retain one month or will they retain 24 months.

487
00:50:13,200 --> 00:50:18,960
And different channels actually do produce very different durations by customers, especially TikTok.

488
00:50:19,120 --> 00:50:27,200
I found to be a flimsier channel in terms of just intent and loyalty or attention span, whatever you want to call it.

489
00:50:27,760 --> 00:50:35,280
So it's really helpful to have annual as a way to add that discipline where upfront you know what your L2B over CAC is at least for the first year.

490
00:50:35,680 --> 00:50:44,400
And also by getting instant CAC payback that gives you the safety that you can then reinvest that money directly into acquiring the next customer.

491
00:50:44,960 --> 00:50:46,160
Yeah, it makes a lot of sense.

492
00:50:46,640 --> 00:50:48,160
Gosh, so much I want to talk about.

493
00:50:48,240 --> 00:50:53,920
Well, let's dive into being a ten percent team and hitting ten millionaire or as a ten percent team.

494
00:50:54,560 --> 00:50:57,040
How do you do that?

495
00:50:57,680 --> 00:50:58,320
It's a good question.

496
00:50:58,880 --> 00:50:59,840
I think it's a mix of things.

497
00:51:00,480 --> 00:51:10,400
Probably the biggest thing I would give credit to is just the people we've hired and how much just everyone is a doer and a owner.

498
00:51:11,120 --> 00:51:30,800
I think everyone here drives a ton of value for the business and part of how we do that is by hiring people who are kind of this founder types who want to have that huge impact and who want to be very ambitious with what they build and who don't require also a lot of, you know, direction and coordination.

499
00:51:31,120 --> 00:51:35,680
They can, you know, are the types of people who can just get things done largely independently.

500
00:51:36,000 --> 00:51:39,840
Another thing that we do as a company is minimizing distractions to building.

501
00:51:40,560 --> 00:51:48,400
So we have one team wide company meeting on Mondays where we just all have an opportunity to sync and everyone is aware of what everyone else is doing.

502
00:51:48,880 --> 00:51:52,640
But other than that, it's really head down time and again, talk to users.

503
00:51:53,120 --> 00:52:02,080
So being very also focused on who the user is, super impactful in having everyone work towards solving that user's problem instead of just building and writing code.

504
00:52:02,480 --> 00:52:04,640
So I think a big part of it is thanks to that.

505
00:52:04,800 --> 00:52:17,520
And of course, also with the latest AI tooling that we have now, right with Cloud, everyone in the company is using Cloud for the first time in my life, I'm actually shipping PRs myself, which is crazy.

506
00:52:17,680 --> 00:52:18,800
I'm not a technical person.

507
00:52:19,440 --> 00:52:24,800
And I'm not here to claim that now I'm the highest, like, producer of code in the company.

508
00:52:24,960 --> 00:52:34,240
I'm definitely the lowest, but just like the amount of like ownership and freedom and and just agency that everyone gets.

509
00:52:34,560 --> 00:52:40,000
And that's just amplified with the fact that this tooling allows us to build even faster.

510
00:52:40,480 --> 00:52:42,160
Another example is our designer.

511
00:52:42,640 --> 00:52:53,440
He has done an incredible job, not just building things on Figma, but translating them to prototypes and even getting them to a almost eighty percent there when it comes to then the engineers taking over and coding it.

512
00:52:53,600 --> 00:53:00,720
So we've also optimized the workflows so that we can get things iterated on and shipped even faster.

513
00:53:01,360 --> 00:53:07,520
I realized as soon as I said that some people are probably going, ten million in ARR, that's not that big and ten people, that's a lot of people.

514
00:53:08,000 --> 00:53:09,120
But it's a very different business.

515
00:53:09,360 --> 00:53:10,640
And that's why it's so impressive.

516
00:53:10,800 --> 00:53:14,720
This is not you spent 15 million on paid ads to get to ten million in ARR.

517
00:53:14,880 --> 00:53:22,320
This is not you had one viral moment and blew up to ten million ARR and you're just going to kind of tank after that viral moment.

518
00:53:22,560 --> 00:53:24,400
It's like you've built a repeatable machine.

519
00:53:25,600 --> 00:53:31,120
learning and AI experts on the team, like you've built a real company and have done so profitably.

520
00:53:31,520 --> 00:53:33,040
So yes, speak to that as well.

521
00:53:33,280 --> 00:53:38,960
Like a lot of people who get to ten million, it's a very different business at the ten million than what you've built.

522
00:53:39,520 --> 00:53:45,680
I feel like, again, we've been around here for five years now, we're not like an overnight success story.

523
00:53:45,840 --> 00:53:56,000
We're also not like an AI rapper where we found like there's this arbitrage, short term arbitrage opportunity of just like putting something out there in front of a user building around a specific model.

524
00:53:56,880 --> 00:54:03,920
And that means that, I mean, hopefully, again, right, like our value is proposition is more durable, our business model is more durable.

525
00:54:04,480 --> 00:54:36,160
The investment we've also done on the AI and ML capabilities has also proven to be the correct investment because still to this day, you can talk to ChatGPT or Cloud or whatever you have and get this kind of feedback that Bold Voice gives you, and we made this bet that when it comes to non native English speakers speaking English, that is a two maybe niche of an opportunity for the more general models to go after, and yet it's a huge opportunity when you just look at one company going after it and how big the market of non native English speakers is.

526
00:54:36,960 --> 00:54:46,320
So yeah, we've invested a lot in, again, building a product that doesn't go away in the next iteration of Chat GPT or cloud.

527
00:54:46,800 --> 00:54:49,360
But of course, the landscape is always changing.

528
00:54:49,600 --> 00:55:05,280
So we're also spending a lot of effort and on the technical side, on the ML side, across the board to continue to innovate and make the product better and better and always a step or hopefully more ahead of where the more general generalized models are.

529
00:55:05,600 --> 00:55:14,000
Well, the last thing I did want to touch on before we wrap up is the kind of shift away from just focusing on accents to kind of a broader mission with the company.

530
00:55:14,240 --> 00:55:16,960
So I wanted to give you a chance to kind of talk through that.

531
00:55:17,440 --> 00:55:17,680
For sure.

532
00:55:18,000 --> 00:55:22,800
So from day one, we've actually called ourselves an accent training app.

533
00:55:23,040 --> 00:55:28,320
And the reason for that is that the vision has always been we need to help people speak more clearly and confidently.

534
00:55:29,120 --> 00:55:34,720
But we also know that the biggest pain point that we're hearing from a lot of people at this last mile of fluency is their accent.

535
00:55:34,960 --> 00:55:42,800
And it seems like such a differentiated and spiky pain point that we can create a product and scale a product initially that focuses on that wedge.

536
00:55:43,200 --> 00:55:48,000
And now we've reached the point in the market in our brand in our revenue as well.

537
00:55:48,720 --> 00:56:00,560
where we and also we raised the series A that allows us to build towards that vision of getting to the next level and using this wedge to expand into this larger market of spoken English fluency.

